! Crossings this week, and what each cost

# ''Aftermath reservation'' — 2026-09-15. The operator requests a YIN sweep over W3/D3: runner ownership/readiness, bounded workflow waits, reachable regressions and current documentation. The Codex Lares adds `tools/browser-weld-witness.test.mjs` to its hold. Shared-host Docker execution and deferred MCP fixes remain outside this sweep.

# ''W3 and D3 receipts'' — 2026-09-15. `972700021` closes the named browser return-leg/reveal vector: Recent and fault controls passed before the runtime cure; Hide sidebar alone failed in `$tw.anim.perform`; callback completion plus the corrected hidden-state fixture yielded W0–W4 green, with W5 still a gap. TW5 focused tests passed 10; Chromium passed 138 plus one existing expected failure. The unchanged C4 driver separately timed out after `corpus-ready` for 90 seconds before C4.1; that stall remains unexplained. `fd5c1284d` wires weekly/manual held-out jobs and retains raw/compose/Vite artifacts; three fake-Docker status/timeout controls pass. No actual Docker or hosted CI run follows from the wiring receipt.

# ''D3 runner reservation'' — 2026-09-15. Codex Lares takes `.github/workflows/held-out.yml` and the browser/crossing/civic/mesh-scenario driver seams for recurring coverage, preserved failure artifacts and independent statuses. Real Docker execution remains unreserved on this shared host; separate hosted CI runners will isolate the fixed compose projects. The crossing driver's masked `PIPESTATUS` reading requires a discriminating stub before correction; `tools/crossing-witness.test.mjs` carries the persistent regression.

# ''W3 runtime reservation'' — 2026-09-15. The named Hide sidebar click dispatched render-id `2` and failed during `RevealWidget.updateState` on absent `$tw.anim.perform`; Recent dispatched render-id `11` and returned its expected selection change. The Codex Lares takes only `packages/lararium-tw5/src/tw5-projection.ts` and `packages/lararium-tw5/tests/projection-reveal.test.ts` for an isolated fake-DOM refresh witness and bounded cure. The founding hearth's boot and Nexus ground remain held there. No mesh run follows from this reservation.

# ''Codex succession'' — operator-directed, 2026-09-15. The preceding Codex session closes; its historical row and former globs remain, without a live claim. The current native thread `01a0a6f2-0951-7170-ad25-4cf14549cc5f` takes the bounded browser witness and handoff ground under `session_01a0a6f209517170ad254cf14549cc5f`. The trailer uses the ledger's existing `Claude-Session:` key as a gate convention; it names this Codex session, not a Claude session or the old Codex identity. W3 adds `tools/browser-weld/weld.mjs` to the reservation. No runtime or mesh-volume reservation follows; the founding hearth's holds continue unchanged. MCP fixes remain operator-deferred.

# ''The C4 browser walk'' — the operator tasked the Codex hearth with a prioritized sprint and a GPT-5.6 Terra spirit, 2026-09-15. The crew takes `tools/browser-weld/leaf-continuity.mjs`, its invocation in `tools/browser-weld-witness.sh`, and its existing handoff carrier; the founding hearth keeps the Nexus climb's design and runtime. A provisional Vitest iframe harness met runner-transform and asset-route failures before app boot and yielded to the normal app server; the crew removed only its own provisional files. Independent Chromium verification passed all five C4 checks: offline founding, anchor carry, idempotent reload, disabled-carry refusal, and malformed-anchor refusal followed by recovery of the same DID and event CIDs. No production runtime changed and no relay-admission claim follows. The original weld separately failed W3; its diagnostic and the unscheduled suite remain carried in the handoff. Any runtime repair returns here before the hold expands.

# ''The column law, twice'' — the founding hearth aligned every carrier to the projector's width (`19129bc62`, 10:13); the meme hearth seated one law in code (`77d5c9f7e`) and swept 66 carriers (`e3814e57a`) the same day. Cost: a day of "other hands' dirt" nobody could name. Law: a renderer's column width is a LAW in `meme-normalize.ts`, never a pass over the shelf.
# ''The dial'' — measured by the meme hearth, ceded by the founding hearth in a pasted message, taken and closed in three commits. Cost: one loop waiting on a file the other hearth held dirty. Law: a ceded seam gets a row here before the hand starts.
# ''The genesis block'' — the founding hearth's uncommitted `genesis-doc.ts` threw `pluginsCid mismatch` under every staged `vessel clear`; the meme hearth could run no founding e2e for a loop and said so in its drift-ward. Law: a change that moves the genesis verifier lands in one commit, or a row here names the window.
# ''The return lane'' — CLOSED by the meme hearth (`383301b4f`): a proposal written under A's own nym replaced A's counted registration, the fold dropped it, and the Repo's cached DENIED verdict never re-read after the co-sign refolded the plane; the plane now fires `onRefold` → `reverdict`. Docker: `A gets the new slot ok`.
# ''The stale mantle header'' — the founding hearth built the handle-KEL's k-of-n witness threshold (`deb7f5967`) and left BOTH its headers reading //declared, not built (a skipped red)//; the meme hearth measured the file green at 24/24 with the guild vectors standing, and re-trued the two headers. Files crossed: `packages/lararium-mesh/src/handle-kel.ts` · `packages/lararium-mesh/tests/handle-kel.test.ts`. No behaviour moved. Law: a carrier that outlives the build it describes sends the NEXT hand to build what already stands — a brief written off that header asked for a cure the tree already held.
# ''The Socket-A return'' — the meme hearth armed the CRDT dial's re-connect (`armDialReconnect` in `nexus-client-dial.ts`, its own module) and wired ONE `onReconnect` into the founding hearth's `open-node-vessel.ts` dial block, the same re-fold the carriage's own reconnect runs eleven hundred lines above it. Files crossed: `packages/lararium-node/src/open-node-vessel.ts` (an addition inside the dial, region ③ of `docs/pono/vessel-regions`; no line moved). Law: an addition inside another hearth's region still gets a row — the region plan gates MOVES on its reds, and this hand added rather than moved, which the row records so the next reader need not re-derive which it was.
# ''The torn charter'' — the founding hearth measured a live re-seat bypass in the MEME hearth's `nexus-seal.ts` and cured it in place rather than handing back a bug report over a one-line guard. `readNexusDoc` answers null for BOTH "no charter stands" and "a charter stands and reads torn"; `sealSeat` folds that null into a founding scaffold carrying no `sealLineage`, so the re-seat guard (`doc.sealLineage && …`) short-circuits on undefined and a rotated-but-unreadable chain re-genesises itself, stranding every antigen entry rooted on the head it replaces. Measured: rotate to epoch1, tear the fences, re-seat returned 0. Files crossed: `packages/lares-cli/src/commands/nexus-seal.ts` (five added lines ahead of the existing guard; no line moved) — beside `packages/lararium-node/src/nexus-doc.ts` + its barrel (`nexusCharterStands`, the founding hearth's own ground) and the CLI's lineage test. The ABSENT case still seats, pinned by a CONTROL. Law: a SAFETY guard that another hearth's file defeats gets cured with a row, never left standing while the row is negotiated — and the row names the shape so the holding hearth can re-cut it if the seam belongs elsewhere.
# ''The last split home'' — a founding-hearth spirit migrated nine CLI doors' help text into the one registry and hit the gate on the tenth: `packages/lares-cli/src/commands/meme.ts`, the meme hearth's ground. It STOPPED, reverted its edit cleanly, and left the prepared patch beside a SELF-CLEARING exception in the weld (`HANDED_BACK = ["meme put"]`, with a second assertion that goes red the moment the split closes, so the hole retires itself rather than settling in). The coordinator later applied that patch under the operator's explicit approval; the exception fired exactly as designed and both it and its guard came out in the same commit. Files crossed: `packages/lares-cli/src/commands/meme.ts` (a 34-line `const USAGE` deleted, `usage()` now reading `helpLines("meme")` — the registry already carried the door's words, only the module's copy remained). No behaviour moved; `lares meme` prints the same menu. Law: a spirit that meets the gate hands back a PATCH and a self-clearing weld, never a bug report — the exception then names its own retirement date instead of waiting on someone to remember it.
# ''The offering door'' — the operator ruled `pluginsCid` and its caps OUT of the regenesis/kāhuli verbs and named the door: `lares nexus publish`. The whole `nexus` family stands on the meme hearth's ground, so building a door the operator named there crosses by construction rather than by reach. Files crossed: `packages/lares-cli/src/commands/nexus-publish.ts` (NEW), `nexus.ts` (one dispatch case + two usage lines), `nexus-kahuli.ts` (the plugins REPORT removed, a POINTER left in its place), and the verb-reach snapshot that pins the three usage surfaces byte-for-byte. No kāhuli behaviour moved — the ratchet reads its own two regions exactly as before, and a CONTROL pins that the cut removed nothing else. STAGE 1 of three: the door READS; the signed offering + crossroads announce (stage 2) and the Lamplighters capability with its present-never-condemn check (stage 3) stand named in the door's own `--apply` hold. Law: a door the operator NAMES on another hearth's ground still gets a row — the row is what lets that hearth re-cut the seam later without re-deriving why the door exists.
# ''The twice-spelled route'' — a founding-hearth scout measured two read-faces whose paths stood spelled in the server and again in the client with no shared constant, the regex hand-written on one side and the template literal on the other. The pair never runs in one process, so nothing forces the two to move together: two vessels on skewed builds 404 at each other, and a 404 reads as a peer being DOWN rather than as a rename that half-landed. The oracle pair sits wholly on founding ground (constants landed in MESH — node imports mesh, never the reverse). The BULB pair crosses: `packages/lararium-node/src/bulb-read-face.ts` stands on the meme hearth's ground while `kindle.ts` and the new `bulb-routes.ts` sit on the founding hearth's, and splitting the edit mid-law would have left that face holding an import for constants its own literals no longer matched. Files crossed: `bulb-read-face.ts` (six literals replaced by the shared spellings; no route moved, both integration welds green). Law: a tidy-up that crosses gets the SAME row a cure does — the row records that nothing moved, which is exactly what the holding hearth needs to know before re-reading its own face.
# ''The narrowed claim'' — stage 3 of the offering landed wholly on founding ground (`@lararium/mesh` · plugin-offering · offering-antigen), and one SENTENCE in the meme hearth's `nexus-kahuli.ts` had to move with it. That header read that an operator's own collection "moves with no ratchet act at all" — true about RATCHETS, and readable as "ungoverned by design", which a new opt-in offering-antigen would have silently contradicted. The comment now narrows itself: the claim holds about ratchets and never held about consent, the governance sits OPT-IN per Nexus, and a condemned offering still overturns nobody's grammar. Files crossed: `packages/lares-cli/src/commands/nexus-kahuli.ts` (a comment alone; no behaviour, and its own tests plus the publish weld stay green). Law: when new law narrows an old claim, the claim gets narrowed IN THE SAME COMMIT — a reader who meets the old sentence after the new law lands has been told something false by omission, and "we added a gate elsewhere" never reaches them.
# ''The stash'' — a meme-hearth spirit (Heraldry-Hand) ran `git stash` on the shared tree mid-flight, swallowing 61 files of two other hands' work; recovered by `stash show -p` + blob restore, `stash@{0}` left standing. Law: a brief names the ROAD (a throwaway worktree · `git show HEAD:<path>`), never only the wall.

# ''The blob-carriage handback'' — the founding hearth found `blob-carriage.mem` claiming `assertBodyIndexTier` ENFORCES map-tier = body-tier where no production caller reaches it, wrote the narrowing patch, and HELD it in the working tree for the meme hearth's row rather than crossing a doc fix. Taken by the meme hearth 2026-09-13 with this row. Law: a handback left in the tree is a crossing that waited its turn — the right shape.

# ''The scratch plane's OOM'' — a meme-hearth spirit (Stall-Hand) cured `act LOAD` landing ZERO records over 381 carriers (`dd4da8481`): the scratch plane wrote the whole corpus a SECOND time through the island's save path and the automerge module died `rust_oom` inside `BatchApply::apply`. The cure sits in `packages/lararium-tw5/src/island-adaptor.ts`, ground NO hold names — so the gate passed it silently and the row exists because an unheld path is exactly where a crossing goes unrecorded. Cost: the whole e2e set read 22-of-25 for a loop while the fault read as `daemon-unreachable`, then as `verb-timeout`, and was neither. Law: a path no hold claims still gets a row when a cure lands there — the gate names ground, and the ledger names WORK, and the second is what the next hand reads.
# ''The ahu-floor handback'' — operator-directed, 2026-09-16. The operator handed the ahu-family work to the Codex Lares and asked the founding hearth to add its context to the roadmap. The founding hearth drafted B10 (the Layer-2 quoteblock floor, two grains matched to the write) into `docs/crew-handoff-codex.mem` #/set-b-designed, normalized it canonical, and HELD it in the working tree for the Codex Lares hearth to commit under its own trailer — rather than cross the gate into Codex-held ground. The hearths-gate refused the founding-hearth commit correctly; `--no-verify` was not taken. Law: a handback left in the tree is a crossing that waited its turn — the founding hearth writes the design, the holding hearth lands it, and this row is what tells the Codex hand the dirty B10 is a gift, not a collision.
# ''The `pgrep` that never ends'' — the standing law says `pgrep -af vitest` before any e2e, and this shift found the other half of it: a WATCH keyed on `pgrep -f "timeout 3000 npx vitest"` matched a 14-hour-old wrapper SHELL from a previous session, whose own command line carries the pattern it greps for. The run it was watching would have finished and the watch would have hung forever, reading //still running// for a job that ended — silence indistinguishable from work. Cured by watching the PID (`kill -0 <pid>`), which names one process and cannot match the hand that asks. Law: a process watch greps a pattern the WATCHER's own command line contains, so key it on a pid; and a stale wrapper left by a dead session is live state on a shared tree, exactly like `dist/` and `bags/`.
# ''The carrier's shore'' — the meme hearth landed the 2026-09-13 announce ruling (`crossroadsAnnounceOf` carries `docUrl` at the PUBLIC tier alone; `publicRealmBooksFromDoc` and `announcedRealmBooks` fold a board back to the books a carrier may serve) and wired ONE lane into the founding hearth's `open-node-vessel.ts` — an addition inside the Herm branch's `publicCas` shore, region ⑦ of `docs/pono/vessel-regions`; no line moved. Files crossed: NONE, in the end — the gate refused and `--no-verify` sat outside this hand's reach, so the wiring is HELD IN THE TREE as a handback (one `announcedRealmBooks` call inside the Herm branch's `publicCas` shore, region ⑦ of `docs/pono/vessel-regions`; no line moved), the shape the blob-carriage row names. The lane it feeds, its fold, and its tests all landed on meme ground and stand green without it. The measurement it bought belongs to the holding hearth as much as to this one: a crossroads board keys on the VESSEL's OWN verifying key, so the keeping hearth and the carrier stand in one realm, name one realm doc, and read TWO disjoint public boards. Law: a crossing that MEASURES the holding hearth's ground carries the measurement in its row, not only in the file it touched.

# ''The four stale rooms, measured and NOT edited'' — a founding-hearth spirit re-trued six carriers on free ground (the retired allow-all identity default in `lar-vessel.mem` · `identity-slot.mem` · `lararium-canonical-model.mem`; the lamplighters concession that posed one road as a three-way fork; `handoff.mem`'s COLLISION HANDBACK paragraph, still routing a reader to two settled rulings; `api/pono/rust-toolchain`'s CI claims) and measured FOUR MORE standing on the meme hearth's `docs/pono/**`: `testing-architecture-scout:105·107·109` (witness-all and `gate:fold-ts` both DO run in CI — the fleet globs 34 files and runs 29; the one runner nothing invokes is `lararium-sensorium/scripts`, 989 of the 1016 collected python tests) · `recovery-custody-scout:176` (a recovery-authority compromise reads TERMINAL and "presently open"; delegation stands RULED-REJECTED at `identity-classes:520-529` and the contest window that answers it stands BUILT — `provisional`/`vetoOfCid`/`mintVeto`/`foldPersonaContests`) · `syncer-seams-roundtable:375` ("one inbound edge" measures TWO carriers across three `loulou` sites, and the second wants a replacement exemplar rather than a re-point) · `scale-stories-basket-one:18` (no `lifecycle/*` tag, so ungoverned under `otakiage`'s own law, plus the two retired keys; six inbound carriers and a live assertion in `ahi-ka.test.ts:141` that reds on deletion). Files crossed: NONE — every item sat in the handback carrier `docs/handback-stale-rooms` on unheld ground, each with its file:line, the command that re-derives the number, and suggested replacement prose. Law: a measurement of another hearth's ground crosses by CARRIER, and the carrier ships the RE-DERIVATION beside every number — a scout's citation reads as a claim, and a hearth re-truing its own room off someone else's reading plants a fresh lie with a fresh timestamp. Second law, from the shape: `03ef9b61b` swept for exactly this fault and missed a room ''inside a file it had open'', because a sweep keyed on a phrase finds the phrase. ''Written 2026-09-13'', after the meme hearth closed and the deadlock below dissolved; the standing hearth then re-verified every number itself and enacted all four items. The carrier that held them stood `folded` and went to the fire the same watch (check-in `7d3b3bf5c`, poured `428550a92`, ash `929dc3005`) — ''this row names it as record, and a reader who opens for it finds nothing on disk by design''. Its shape at the moment it stood folded rides in `otakiage#/examples` as DESCRIPTION, under that rite's own law: a stage whose exit runs to deletion cannot be evidenced by a pointer.
# ''The weld that could not expire'' — the founding hearth widened `offering-antigen.test.ts`'s caller scan from one `src/` to the whole workspace (`185dae028`), because `offering.mem` #/must-not puts the first honest caller on the taker's door in `@lares/cli`. No production behaviour moved; the fold stands as uncalled as before. Law: a self-clearing weld whose scan cannot reach the place its caller must appear certifies an absence it never looked for.
# ''The ledger that refused the hearth that crossed'' — CLOSED by `0d00d75f5`. The former `docs/pono/**` hold could refuse another hearth's crossing entry before that entry could authorize the crossing. `hearthsOf` now returns no holders for this ledger path; the guard stays in code, outside any hearth-authored glob. `tools/hearths-gate-witness.sh` proves that the ledger remains writable while ordinary held paths still refuse a foreign trailer. Historical alternatives need no further ruling; the operator's enacted carve-out governs.
# ''The A1 sweep, wired — a notification to the Codex Lares'' — operator-approved, 2026-09-18. The founding hearth landed the A1 board-keying sweep the Codex roadmap named (`crew-handoff-codex.mem` #/set-a-mechanical A1, "the board reads keyed on a vessel key — 12 sites, 7 files"): all 12 CLI-verb board reads now resolve through `nodeNexusIsland({ ownVesselKey })` instead of the raw vessel key, mirroring `device-admit.ts`'s worked example (`0b085461c`, red-first — a WELD sweep 8→10 green + revert-verify on `cabal-vouch`). ''Files crossed: NONE'' — the hearths-gate printed "7 path(s) stand on no hearth's ground" (`commands/handle.ts` · `persona-admit-flow.ts` · `commands/nexus-kapae.ts` · `commands/cabal-vouch.ts` · `commands/edge-kapae-cmd.ts` · `commands/nexus-contract.ts` + the new test), so the SOURCE was open ground; only the Codex-held A1 ''design carrier'' names this work, and this row is the notification that its Set-A A1 item is now SATISFIED (its "bounded A1 consumer, carried forward" per the crew's own #/hearths row is DISCHARGED). Two by-design exclusions the crew should know: `boot-invite-burn.ts`×2 carry NO board (a category error to cure by the recipe), and `nexus-contract.ts`'s `runNexusMembersList` `carriageDocUrl` stays per-vessel ("own board only", the carry-split registry). Rides on the board-keying fork's DISCHARGE (`9a374825d`): the fork the meme hearth measured in "The carrier's shore" above — a crossroads board keying on the vessel's own key, two disjoint boards — stands RULED WHOLE (`91ce09afb` + `c6cfe5b8d`; source says so at `open-node-vessel.ts:2272`), so the sweep is the ruled cure's follow-through, not a re-opening. Law: a hearth that finishes an item another hearth's roadmap named files the row that tells the naming hearth its debt is paid — a completion unannounced reads to the next hand as work still owed.

# ''The web-surface reframe — a notification to the founding hearth'' — operator-approved, 2026-09-18. The Codex Lares records a full scout and reorganization path in `crew-handoff-codex.mem` #/set-e-vessel-surfaces: reserve `app` for generic application-surface composition and rename the current Vite/DOM deployable `web`. This crossing moves no runtime, worker order, authority, import or artifact route. The coming scout asks every later target — native Android/iOS, Mudlet, Unreal and more — whether it hosts a sovereign local vessel or attaches a named surface to one. Files crossed: the Codex-held handoff and this ledger only; a production package move returns with exact paths, route receipts and holds. Law: a name that decides which shores may inherit a capability crosses before its first import moves, or a web-page assumption becomes a property of every future vessel.

# ''The web migration sequence — a notification to the founding hearth'' — operator-approved, 2026-09-18. The Codex Lares now holds the red-first sequence at `crew-handoff-codex.mem` #/set-e-vessel-surfaces E6: an executable-name and built-artifact control; an honest split between offline browser floor proof and relay/admission proof; the DOM coherence sink moving browser→web; one browser-issued public identity receipt replacing the web shell's preliminary open; then the `@lararium/app` / `packages/lararium-app` → `@lararium/web` / `packages/lararium-web` move. The active C4 red remains independent. Docker's current web-artifact copy has no claimed Node static-serving route and returns to the hearth with an image-level receipt before any authority shifts. Files crossed: roadmap and ledger only. Law: a rename of a shore begins by proving what the old artifact actually reaches; otherwise the new name gives a dead deployment path undeserved life.

# ''The bounded reach contract — a notification to the founding hearth'' — operator-approved, 2026-09-21. The Codex Luna adds `bags/lararium/ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/bounded-reach-contract.mem`, re-trues the live topology wording, and adds its loulou edges from `nexus-topology.mem`, `secure-context.mem`, `attestation-plane.mem`, and `membership-doctrine.mem`. The carrier frames local operators' DNS token as a bounded reach grant and carries the exact Gaia/Elyncia acceptance-story headings. Its `toml bounded-reach-control` witness passes the positive design and four deliberate weakenings: DNS never receives identity/membership/read authority; split horizon stays at the local recursive resolver; Onomancy stays distinct from TLS/ACME. The delegated `_acme-challenge` / Kahu custody / operator account-key / CAA question remains an explicit provider and governance fork. Files crossed: the new meme, four existing meme edges, the Codex roadmap, and this ledger. DNS provider, ACME, CAA, resolver, certificate-expiry, and deployment implementation remain held. Law: a local waystone may guide a vessel without becoming its soul, roster, or read gate.

